<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles I of Hungary</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1288">1288</a>–<a href="/years/1342">1342</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Charles Robert of Anjou · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-house-of-anjou">Capetian House of Anjou</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, also called Charles Robert, was the first Angevin king of Hungary, who won the throne after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpad">Árpád</a> line died out in 1301. By 1321 he had broken the power of great oligarchs such as Matthew Csák. He reformed royal finances and introduced the gold florin, minted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kremnica">Kremnica</a> gold, and allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-hungary.jpg" alt="Charles I of Hungary" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Márk Kálti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C3%A9pes_kr%C3%B3nika_-_138.oldal_-_K%C3%A1roly_R%C3%B3bert_kir%C3%A1ly.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles was born in 1288 as Caroberto, the only son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a>, Prince of Salerno, and Clemence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. His grandmother Mary, daughter of Stephen V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, claimed Hungary after her brother Ladislaus IV died in 1290, though the lords and prelates chose Andrew III instead. She passed the claim to her son and, after his death in 1295, to the seven-year-old Charles. His grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-naples">Charles II of Naples</a> preferred his third son, Robert, and in 1296 gave him the rights of a firstborn, which excluded Charles from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>.</p><p>In August 1300 the twelve-year-old Charles landed at Split, invited by the Croatian lord Paul Šubić. After Andrew III died on 14 January 1301 he was crowned with a provisional crown at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Esztergom</a>, but most Hungarians held the coronation unlawful and elected Wenceslaus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Charles was elected king in Pest on 27 November 1308 and crowned with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-of-thorns">Holy Crown</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szekesfehervar">Székesfehérvár</a> on 27 August 1310, his third coronation, yet his rule stayed nominal over most of the kingdom, which had broken into about a dozen provinces under oligarchs. He won his first decisive victory at Rozgony on 15 June 1312 against the Aba family and Matthew Csák. By 1323 he had taken full possession of his kingdom and moved his capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visegrad">Visegrád</a>. Croatia kept its autonomous status.</p><p>Charles introduced a system of office <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a>, under which officials enjoyed revenues only while they held a royal office, and founded the Order of Saint George, the first secular order of knights. He promoted new gold mines, and the first Hungarian gold coins were minted in his reign. In 1330 his army was ambushed by the Wallachians at Posada, and Wallachia became independent. At the congress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visegrad">Visegrád</a> in 1335 he mediated between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-bohemia">John of Bohemia</a> and Casimir III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Charles died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visegrad">Visegrád</a> on 16 July 1342 and was buried in the Székesfehérvár Basilica. He married several times: historians disagree about his first wife, Mary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bytom">Bytom</a> or Mary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, and he then married Beatrice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> and, in 1320, Elisabeth, daughter of Władysław I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis the Great</a> succeeded him.</p><p>He declared that his aim was the restoration of the ancient good conditions of the kingdom. He left his son a full treasury and an effective system of taxation, according to Bryan Cartledge, but Louis's achievements overshadowed his reputation. The only contemporary record of his deeds was written by a hostile Franciscan friar, who dwelt on his cruel revenge against the family of Felician Záh and called the defeat at Posada a punishment from God.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles I of Hungary” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Charles Robert of Anjou</b>, a descendant through the female line, Wenceslaus of Bohemia and Otto of Bavaria all claimed the throne.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Charles Robert of Anjou</b>, grandson of Mary of Hungary, a daughter of Stephen V, was backed by the papacy but opposed first by Wenceslaus of Bohemia, crowned in 1301, and then by Otto of Bavaria, crowned in 1305.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When he refused to hand Făgăraș, Almaș and Severin to <b>Charles I of Hungary</b> and to pay tribute, the king invaded and was decisively defeated at the Battle of Posada, which consolidated the Wallachian state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-wallachia">Principality of Wallachia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>King <b>Charles I of Hungary</b> defeated the sons of the late Palatine Amadeus Aba, backed by troops of Matthew Csák, near Rozgony.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-15">15 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Battle of Posada, fought between 9 and 12 November, Wallachians under the voivode Basarab I ambushed and nearly annihilated the invading army of <b>Charles I of Hungary</b>, ending Hungarian suzerainty in Wallachia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-12">12 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visegrad">Visegrád</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.84,14.25&amp;year=1323" aria-label="Naples on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4899 706 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Charles I of Hungary"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5349 976h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5349 976h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.84,14.25&amp;year=1323">What was here in 1323? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
